pcm: do the per-tick work once per tick, not once per slot

Five changes to the PCM chip's inner loop that share one idea: work that is
constant across the 32 voice slots, or across the four address-step
iterations, or provably dead below a threshold, was being redone at every
site. None of it changes what is computed, only how often.

  * calc_tv's per-tick invariants -- the tap table and a 256-entry type
    table -- are built once per tick instead of 97 recomputations per tick.
  * The address step is 0, -1 or +1 with a per-slot sign, so the arithmetic
    that derived it folds to boolean algebra, and reg_slots is hoisted.
  * calc_tv is templated on the envelope index: at all four call sites the
    index is a literal, and the e == 2 instantiation drops its dead half.
  * Only 6 of 32 slots inject into the reverb bus; two indirect jumps per
    slot become one predictable test -- worth more than its instruction
    count on an in-order core, where an indirect branch is expensive.
  * Below the sub_phase_of threshold the fourth address step is dead, and
    with it one of the five wave-ROM reads per slot.

Together they are -21.0% of PCM per tick with all 32 voices sounding, and
between -19.7% and -22.7% across every activity level -- the saving does
not depend on how busy the synth is. On real mk2 ROMs they are 11.2% of the
whole emulator's retired instructions. Equivalence: pcm.cpp built twice into
a driver that digests the entire mutable PCM state each tick -- ram1, ram2,
all of eram, every scalar, every sample and every interrupt -- over 44 cases
in 10 modes, including two wide-pitch modes added specifically so that the
fourth-step branch is exercised; see patches/tests/pcm/.

